Step 3: Hardening SSH Daemon Configuration
Modify the OpenSSH server daemon configuration file to enforce strict security policies:

# Backup original configuration
sudo cp /etc/ssh/sshd_config /etc/ssh/sshd_config.bak

# Edit sshd_config
sudo nano /etc/ssh/sshd_config

Apply the following hardening directives inside /etc/ssh/sshd_config:

# 1. Change default port to reduce automated bot scan noise
Port 2222

# 2. Disable root login over SSH
PermitRootLogin no

# 3. Disable password authentication (Enforce SSH Keys only)
PasswordAuthentication no
ChallengeResponseAuthentication no
PubkeyAuthentication yes

# 4. Limit maximum authentication attempts per connection
MaxAuthTries 3

# 5. Disable empty passwords and X11 forwarding
PermitEmptyPasswords no
X11Forwarding no


Step 4: Configure Uncomplicated Firewall (UFW)
Before restarting SSH, configure UFW  so you don't lock yourself out!

# Set default traffic policies
sudo ufw default deny incoming
sudo ufw default allow outgoing

# Allow custom SSH port (Port 2222)
sudo ufw allow 2222/tcp comment 'Custom SSH Port'

# Allow standard web ports (for future application deployment)
sudo ufw allow 80/tcp comment 'HTTP'
sudo ufw allow 443/tcp comment 'HTTPS'

# Enable firewall
sudo ufw enable

# Verify active rules
sudo ufw status verbose


Step 5: Restart SSH & Test Connections
Apply the SSH changes and verify connection status:

